{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TN3QYL7HMtzhEUHaWX6opM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've now read the full paper and the review context YAML. Let me understand what I need to do:\n\n1. This is a DREAM review in \"full\" mode\n2. I need to dispatch 2 Reviewers: R1 (methods + experiments) and R2 (novelty + related work)\n3. Full roundtable: Step 1 independent → Step 2 cross-share → Step 3 converge → Step 4 synthesize\n4. The paper is about VLA fine-tuning teaching routing rather than representation\n5. The review context tells me this is the \"worst\" tier - but I must NOT mention this to reviewers\n\n\n6. Now I'm dispatching both reviewers in parallel for their independent analyses of the paper's three main claims about SigLIP alignment, LLM effects on geometric structure, and fine-tuning's role in learning routing rather than representations, along with the metric dissociation findings and cross-benchmark validation.\n\nThe experimental plan spans 11 completed investigations covering RSA comparisons across architectures, per-layer degradation patterns, output head analysis, multi-method probing across benchmarks, instruction ablations, training-instruction matrices, control conditions, CKA verification, and cross-benchmark generalization, though the broader probing study was orphaned while its sub-experiments succeeded.\n\nNow I'm cross-referencing these experiments against the paper's actual content—most appear covered across the tables and analysis sections, though some details like the full six-method probing breakdown aren't individually displayed.\n\nThe CLIP-ViT pretraining control is presented as two hypothetical scenarios rather than concrete experimental results, which seems like a methodological gap. I'm trying to parse whether they actually tested multiple encoder variants or if they're just speculating about possible outcomes.\n\nNow I need to set up the parallel review process—I'll prepare detailed prompts for both reviewers, with R1 focusing on methods and experiments while R2 examines novelty and related work, then have them each provide scores across the standard dimensions.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":29740,"cache_read_input_tokens":22929,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":29740},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eb31c56b-0827-4300-bb16-02d899856f0f","uuid":"a9ddbeb6-9376-4692-9453-c3cfc4dee238"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TN3QYL7HMtzhEUHaWX6opM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paper and context read. Dispatching R1 (methods + experiments) and R2 (novelty + related work) in parallel for Step 1 independent reviews."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":29740,"cache_read_input_tokens":22929,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":29740},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eb31c56b-0827-4300-bb16-02d899856f0f","uuid":"7602c306-b964-48e7-b00f-12252fce473b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TN3QYL7HMtzhEUHaWX6opM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01G9mp9xmjhnKJ393kxGNeHB","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus: **methods soundness and experimental rigor**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Fine-Tuning Teaches Routing, Not Representation: A Multi-Metric Dissociation Analysis of Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\nThe paper analyzes OpenVLA's representations across vision encoder (SigLIP), projector, and LLM backbone, using RSA, CKA, and linear R² probes. Three claims:\n1. SigLIP provides highest geometric alignment (RSA=0.615) but CLIP-ViT control shows this is SigLIP-specific, not VL-pretraining general (CLIP-ViT RSA≈0.39 ≈ DINOv2=0.388).\n2. LLM degrades geometric alignment (RSA: 0.615→0.501; R²: 0.706→0.683) but learns readout concentration (R²=0.731 vs untrained 0.686, Δ=0.045, p<0.0001).\n3. Fine-tuning leaves distributed representations invariant (trained vs untrained RSA: 0.501 vs 0.485, p=0.20; confirmed by 6 probing methods, 3 instruction conditions). Training only teaches routing.\n\nAdditional findings: RSA/CKA/R² yield inconsistent model rankings (triple metric dissociation). LIBERO-Spatial cross-benchmark shows routing is distribution-specific (invariance breaks at p=0.02).\n\n## Key Tables\n- Table 1: RSA across conditions. SigLIP 0.615, Trained 0.501, Untrained 0.485, DINO 0.388, CLIP-ViT Scenario A 0.39, Scenario B 0.62\n- Table 2: Scenario A — CLIP-ViT RSA=0.39, R²=0.69; SigLIP RSA=0.615, R²=0.706; DINO RSA=0.388, R²=0.712\n- Table 3: Scenario B — CLIP-ViT RSA=0.62, R²=0.69; SigLIP RSA=0.615, R²=0.706; DINO RSA=0.388, R²=0.712\n- Table 4: Per-layer RSA — Proj 0.615, Proj→L1 drop 20-40%, Peak LLM 0.488, non-monotonic, all LLM > DINO\n- Table 5: 2×2 factorial (training × instruction): Trained+inst=0.488, Trained-no_inst=0.489, Untrained+inst=0.485, Untrained-no_inst=0.480\n- Table 6: Instruction content control: Normal=0.489, Random=0.484, Shuffled=0.483, Empty=0.472, Max|Δ|=0.017\n- Table 7: R² — SigLIP+Proj=0.706, DINO=0.712, LLM img-mean=0.683, Trained readout=0.731, Untrained readout=0.686\n- Table 8: Triple dissociation — RSA: SigLIP>trained>untrained>DINO; CKA: untrained>trained>SigLIP>DINO; R²: readout>DINO≈SigLIP>LLM mean\n- Table 9: LIBERO-Spatial — SigLIP RSA 0.42 (vs 0.615), Trained 0.38 (vs 0.501), Untrained 0.40 (vs 0.485), Training Δ p=0.02 (vs 0.20), Readout Δ 0.010 (vs 0.045)\n\n## Experiment Coverage Reference\nThe full experiment plan includes 12 experiments (plan_001 through plan_012). The paper covers all of them, but note how the CLIP-ViT control (plan_012) is presented — it reports TWO hypothetical scenarios rather than one actual result. The six probing methods (plan_005) are referenced but not individually detailed.\n\n## Your Task\nEvaluate methods and experiments rigorously. Address:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the decomposition (SigLIP / projector / LLM) well-motivated? Are RSA, CKA, R² well-chosen and correctly applied? Is image-mean aggregation appropriate? Is the statistical methodology (bootstrap 10K, CIs) adequate?\n\n2. **Experimental rigor**: \n   - The CLIP-ViT control presents two \"scenarios\" (A and B) in a single paper — did they actually run one experiment and get one result, or is this presenting hypothetical outcomes? This is extremely unusual for empirical work.\n   - Is 500 episodes / 10K pairs sufficient for the statistical claims?\n   - The trained vs untrained difference (0.501 vs 0.485, p=0.20) — is the power adequate to claim \"no difference\"? (Absence of evidence vs evidence of absence)\n   - LIBERO-Spatial breaks the invariance (p=0.02) — does this undermine the core thesis?\n   - Are there missing controls or ablations?\n\n3. **Claim calibration**: Are the claims well-supported by the evidence? Any overclaiming?\n\n4. **Presentation**: Tables, clarity, reproducibility.\n\nProvide:\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, numbered W1, W2, ...)\n- Questions for authors (numbered Q1, Q2, ...)\n- Minor issues\n- Score (1-6) and confidence (1-5)\n- One-line summary recommendation\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":29740,"cache_read_input_tokens":22929,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":29740},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eb31c56b-0827-4300-bb16-02d899856f0f","uuid":"c7511caf-1f3b-4a37-b6cf-21c322f94986"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a1fd31ec7400c9c9b","tool_use_id":"toolu_01G9mp9xmjhnKJ393kxGNeHB","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus: **methods soundness and experimental rigor**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Fine-Tuning Teaches Routing, Not Representation: A Multi-Metric Dissociation Analysis of Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\nThe paper analyzes OpenVLA's representations across vision encoder (SigLIP), projector, and LLM backbone, using RSA, CKA, and linear R² probes. Three claims:\n1. SigLIP provides highest geometric alignment (RSA=0.615) but CLIP-ViT control shows this is SigLIP-specific, not VL-pretraining general (CLIP-ViT RSA≈0.39 ≈ DINOv2=0.388).\n2. LLM degrades geometric alignment (RSA: 0.615→0.501; R²: 0.706→0.683) but learns readout concentration (R²=0.731 vs untrained 0.686, Δ=0.045, p<0.0001).\n3. Fine-tuning leaves distributed representations invariant (trained vs untrained RSA: 0.501 vs 0.485, p=0.20; confirmed by 6 probing methods, 3 instruction conditions). Training only teaches routing.\n\nAdditional findings: RSA/CKA/R² yield inconsistent model rankings (triple metric dissociation). LIBERO-Spatial cross-benchmark shows routing is distribution-specific (invariance breaks at p=0.02).\n\n## Key Tables\n- Table 1: RSA across conditions. SigLIP 0.615, Trained 0.501, Untrained 0.485, DINO 0.388, CLIP-ViT Scenario A 0.39, Scenario B 0.62\n- Table 2: Scenario A — CLIP-ViT RSA=0.39, R²=0.69; SigLIP RSA=0.615, R²=0.706; DINO RSA=0.388, R²=0.712\n- Table 3: Scenario B — CLIP-ViT RSA=0.62, R²=0.69; SigLIP RSA=0.615, R²=0.706; DINO RSA=0.388, R²=0.712\n- Table 4: Per-layer RSA — Proj 0.615, Proj→L1 drop 20-40%, Peak LLM 0.488, non-monotonic, all LLM > DINO\n- Table 5: 2×2 factorial (training × instruction): Trained+inst=0.488, Trained-no_inst=0.489, Untrained+inst=0.485, Untrained-no_inst=0.480\n- Table 6: Instruction content control: Normal=0.489, Random=0.484, Shuffled=0.483, Empty=0.472, Max|Δ|=0.017\n- Table 7: R² — SigLIP+Proj=0.706, DINO=0.712, LLM img-mean=0.683, Trained readout=0.731, Untrained readout=0.686\n- Table 8: Triple dissociation — RSA: SigLIP>trained>untrained>DINO; CKA: untrained>trained>SigLIP>DINO; R²: readout>DINO≈SigLIP>LLM mean\n- Table 9: LIBERO-Spatial — SigLIP RSA 0.42 (vs 0.615), Trained 0.38 (vs 0.501), Untrained 0.40 (vs 0.485), Training Δ p=0.02 (vs 0.20), Readout Δ 0.010 (vs 0.045)\n\n## Experiment Coverage Reference\nThe full experiment plan includes 12 experiments (plan_001 through plan_012). The paper covers all of them, but note how the CLIP-ViT control (plan_012) is presented — it reports TWO hypothetical scenarios rather than one actual result. The six probing methods (plan_005) are referenced but not individually detailed.\n\n## Your Task\nEvaluate methods and experiments rigorously. Address:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the decomposition (SigLIP / projector / LLM) well-motivated? Are RSA, CKA, R² well-chosen and correctly applied? Is image-mean aggregation appropriate? Is the statistical methodology (bootstrap 10K, CIs) adequate?\n\n2. **Experimental rigor**: \n   - The CLIP-ViT control presents two \"scenarios\" (A and B) in a single paper — did they actually run one experiment and get one result, or is this presenting hypothetical outcomes? This is extremely unusual for empirical work.\n   - Is 500 episodes / 10K pairs sufficient for the statistical claims?\n   - The trained vs untrained difference (0.501 vs 0.485, p=0.20) — is the power adequate to claim \"no difference\"? (Absence of evidence vs evidence of absence)\n   - LIBERO-Spatial breaks the invariance (p=0.02) — does this undermine the core thesis?\n   - Are there missing controls or ablations?\n\n3. **Claim calibration**: Are the claims well-supported by the evidence? Any overclaiming?\n\n4. **Presentation**: Tables, clarity, reproducibility.\n\nProvide:\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, numbered W1, W2, ...)\n- Questions for authors (numbered Q1, Q2, ...)\n- Minor issues\n- Score (1-6) and confidence (1-5)\n- One-line summary recommendation\n","uuid":"2d53e804-9348-4698-9d73-010a05876967","session_id":"eb31c56b-0827-4300-bb16-02d899856f0f"}
