Primary benchmark: LIBERO-Goal (500 episodes, 10K+ pairs)\n- Cross-benchmark: LIBERO-Spatial\n- CLIP-ViT control: presented as two hypothetical \"scenarios\" (A: RSA≈0.39 ≈ DINO; B: RSA≈0.62 > SigLIP)\n- DINOv2 as non-VL baseline\n- Bootstrap resampling (10K iterations) for statistics\n\n## References (12 total)\n[1] Brohan et al. RT-2 (CoRL 2023)\n[2] Fang (2025) fine-tuning erodes reasoning\n[3] Fei et al. LIBERO-Plus (2025)\n[4] Grant et al. visual pathway dominance (2026)\n[5] Haon et al. mechanistic interpretability (CoRL 2025)\n[6] Hou. LangGap (2026)\n[7] Karamcheti et al. Prismatic VLMs (ICML 2024)\n[8] Kim et al. OpenVLA (CoRL 2024)\n[9] Lu et al. linear probing (2025)\n[10] Molinari et al. emergent world representations (2025)\n[11] Rizzardo et al. feature observability (2026)\n[12] Swann et al. sparse autoencoders (2026)\n\n## Your Task\nEvaluate novelty, significance, and related work positioning rigorously:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**:\n   - Is \"routing not creating\" genuinely novel, or is it a restatement of known observations (e.g., frozen encoder dominance from Grant et al. 2026, instruction ignoring from Hou 2026)?\n   - How novel is the multi-metric dissociation finding? RSA/CKA disagreement is known in representation learning literature. Is extending it to VLAs a sufficient contribution?\n   - Is the paper primarily an empirical analysis or does it offer mechanistic insight beyond measurement?\n   - The CLIP-ViT control is potentially the most novel element but is presented as hypothetical scenarios — does this reduce its contribution?\n\n2. **Significance and impact**:\n   - Will this change how people build VLAs? The paper claims \"lightweight routing mechanisms could replace full LLM fine-tuning\" but provides no evidence for this.\n   - Is the finding that fine-tuning doesn't change distributed representations surprising or expected? (Frozen vision encoder → frozen early representations is somewhat tautological since SigLIP+projector are frozen.)\n   - Does the LIBERO-Spatial result (routing breaks OOD) actually weaken the paper's own thesis?\n\n3. **Related work gaps**:\n   - Only 12 references — is this sufficient for a venue paper?\n   - Missing comparisons: What about representation analyses in NLP (e.g., probing the role of fine-tuning in language models)? Transfer learning literature on feature reuse vs. learning?\n   - No discussion of Kornblith et al. (CKA), Kriegeskorte et al. (RSA) as foundational metric references.\n   - No comparison with other VLA architectures beyond OpenVLA.\n\n4. **Framing and positioning**: Is the paper positioned accurately? Does the title/abstract promise more than the paper delivers?\n\nProvide:\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, numbered W1, W2, ...)\n- Questions for authors (numbered Q1, Q2, ...)\n- Minor issues\n- Score (1-6) and confidence (1-5)\n- One-line summary recommendation\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01ToAgWBdV6Hw4hJuo3s1wxf","session_id":"eb31c56b-0827-4300-bb16-02d899856f0f","uuid":"429b360d-875c-468d-af93-c3964f22c1d8","timestamp":"2026-05-16T01:55:19.456Z"}
