{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WJWuC8BW2RuzjDyoNrrXGt","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/writing_plan.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"venue: CoRL 2026\\\\nabstract_ddl: 2026-05-26\\\\npaper_ddl: 2026-05-29\\\\nformat: \\\\\\\"8 pages + references\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\ntitle_candidates:\\\\n  - \\\\\\\"Where Do Action Representations Come From? Dissecting Vision-Language-Action Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - \\\\\\\"The Frozen Encoder Provides, the LLM Routes: Representational Analysis of Vision-Language-Action Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - \\\\\\\"Vision Encodes, Language Routes: A Representational Decomposition of VLA Action Knowledge\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# SECTIONS\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\nsections:\\\\n\\\\n  - name: Abstract\\\\n    core_evidence:\\\\n      - d027_siglip_only_rsa          # RSA=0.615 smoking gun\\\\n      - p0_functional_action_probe    # R² projector 0.706, readout 0.731\\\\n      - p0_bootstrap_ci_training_effect  # training invariance CI includes 0\\\\n      - untrained_last_preaction_probe   # readout trained-dependent Δ=0.045\\\\n    key_numbers:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"SigLIP+projector RSA ρ=0.615 vs full VLA ρ≈0.50 vs DINO ρ=0.388\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Projector action R²=0.706; LLM image_mean R²=0.683 (degradation); readout R²=0.731 (concentration)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Training effect on distributed RSA: Δ=0.021, CI=[−0.031, +0.064], n.s.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Training effect on readout R²: Δ=0.045, CI=[0.032, 0.058], p<0.0001\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Untrained readout R²=0.686 < projector 0.706 — redistribution is learned\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - name: Introduction\\\\n    structure:\\\\n      - para_1_problem: >\\\\n          VLAs achieve impressive manipulation performance by fine-tuning LLMs on robot data.\\\\n          Implicit assumption: fine-tuning injects action-relevant representational knowledge.\\\\n          But where do action representations actually come from?\\\\n      - para_2_gap: >\\\\n          Grant et al. 2026 showed visual pathway dominates at behavioral level.\\\\n          Hou 2026 / Fei 2025 showed VLAs largely ignore language instructions.\\\\n          But no work has performed representational decomposition — measuring each component's\\\\n          independent contribution to action-relevant structure.\\\\n      - para_3_approach: >\\\\n          We systematically decompose OpenVLA: extract representations from\\\\n          (1) frozen SigLIP+projector, (2) LLM at every layer, (3) readout token.\\\\n          Compare trained vs untrained LLM × with/without instruction, using RSA (geometric alignment)\\\\n          and functional probes (action prediction R²).\\\\n      - para_4_findings: >\\\\n          Three findings: (1) Frozen vision encoder provides dominant action-relevant structure\\\\n          (RSA=0.615 >> VLA 0.50 >> DINO 0.388). (2) LLM degrades distributed geometric alignment\\\\n          but concentrates signal into readout token via training-dependent attention.\\\\n          (3) Training teaches routing, not creating — distributed structure is training-invariant.\\\\n      - para_5_implications: >\\\\n          Implications for VLA design: vision encoder pretraining matters most;\\\\n          LLM's role is information routing not knowledge injection;\\\\n          VL pretraining provides geometric alignment beyond raw information content.\\\\n    core_evidence:\\\\n      - d027_siglip_only_rsa\\\\n      - p0_functional_probe_v2   # DINO ≈ Proj in R²\\\\n      - p0_bootstrap_ci_training_effect\\\\n\\\\n  - name: Related Work\\\\n    categories:\\\\n      - name: \\\\\\\"Vision-Language-Action Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n        papers:\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Karamcheti et al. 2024 (Prismatic/OpenVLA) — architectural foundation\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Kim et al. 2024 (OpenVLA) — model we analyze\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Brohan et al. 2023 (RT-2) — VLA paradigm origin\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - name: \\\\\\\"Behavioral Analysis of VLAs\\\\\\\"\\\\n        papers:\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Grant et al. 2026 — visual pathway dominance (behavioral level)\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Hou 2026 (LangGap) — VLAs ignore language instructions\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Fei 2025 (LIBERO-Plus) — language-conditioned evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - name: \\\\\\\"Mechanistic Interpretability of VLAs\\\\\\\"\\\\n        papers:\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Haon et al. CoRL 2025 — activation steering on OpenVLA\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Swann et al. 2026 — SAE-based OpenVLA interpretability\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Rizzardo et al. 2026 — feature observability/controllability\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Molinari et al. 2025 — emergent world representations\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Lu et al. 2025 — linear probing OpenVLA hidden layers\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - name: \\\\\\\"Vision Encoder Representations\\\\\\\"\\\\n        papers:\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Fang 2025 — fine-tuning erodes VLM reasoning\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Oquab et al. 2024 (DINOv2) — self-supervised vision baseline\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Zhai et al. 2023 (SigLIP) — vision-language pretraining\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - name: \\\\\\\"Representational Similarity Analysis\\\\\\\"\\\\n        papers:\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Kriegeskorte et al. 2008 — RSA framework\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Kornblith et al. 2019 — CKA\\\\\\\"\\\\n    positioning: >\\\\n      Grant et al. 2026 is closest — we confirm their behavioral finding at representation level\\\\n      with quantitative component decomposition. Lu et al. 2025 methodologically closest —\\\\n      we differentiate via component-level (not just layer-level) analysis and causal controls\\\\n      (trained/untrained × instruction ablation). Haon et al. same venue+model —\\\\n      complementary (sparse semantic directions vs geometric analysis).\\\\n\\\\n  - name: Method\\\\n    subsections:\\\\n      - name: \\\\\\\"2.1 Model and Data\\\\\\\"\\\\n        content_outline: >\\\\n          OpenVLA architecture: SigLIP-SO400M → MLP projector → Llama-2 7B → action head.\\\\n          LIBERO-10 fine-tuned checkpoint (10 tasks, 50 demos/task).\\\\n          LIBERO-Goal as OOD evaluation benchmark (shared initial states, different goals).\\\\n          Untrained control: from_config() reinitializes LLM (6.74B random), preserves SigLIP (0.80B).\\\\n      - name: \\\\\\\"2.2 Representation Extraction\\\\\\\"\\\\n        content_outline: >\\\\n          Four representation sources: (a) SigLIP+projector output (before LLM),\\\\n          (b) LLM per-layer image_mean (mean of image tokens at each layer),\\\\n          (c) LLM per-layer last_preaction (readout token = last token before action prediction),\\\\n          (d) DINOv2 cls_token (vision-only baseline).\\\\n          2×2 conditions: trained/untrained × with-instruction/no-instruction.\\\\n      - name: \\\\\\\"2.3 Representational Similarity Analysis (RSA)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        content_outline: >\\\\n          Construct RDMs for representations and 7-D action space.\\\\n          Spearman correlation between upper-triangular RDM vectors.\\\\n          Task-level analysis with per-task RSA + cross-task aggregation.\\\\n          Bootstrap CI (10,000 resamples) for pairwise comparisons.\\\\n      - name: \\\\\\\"2.4 Functional Action Probes\\\\\\\"\\\\n        content_outline: >\\\\n          MLP probe (Linear→ReLU→Linear, input_dim→256→7; Adam lr=1e-3, batch=256,\\\\n          epochs=50, early stopping patience=5) trained on representation→action mapping.\\\\n          Mean R² across 7 action dimensions. Per-task train/val splits.\\\\n          Bootstrap paired comparison for statistical significance.\\\\n          Also: action-delta probes (predict Δaction = a_{t+1} - a_t).\\\\n      - name: \\\\\\\"2.5 CKA Analysis\\\\\\\"\\\\n        content_outline: >\\\\n          Linear CKA and RDM-based CKA as complementary metric to RSA.\\\\n          Motivation: RSA captures local geometric alignment, CKA captures global structure.\\\\n          Note dimension mismatch caveat for linear CKA (4096-D repr vs 7-D action).\\\\n\\\\n  - name: Experiments\\\\n    subsections:\\\\n      - name: \\\\\\\"3.1 Experimental Setup\\\\\\\"\\\\n        content_outline: >\\\\n          LIBERO-Goal benchmark: 10 manipulation tasks, 50 demos each, 500 frames per task.\\\\n          Representation extraction pipeline. Statistical testing protocol (bootstrap, permutation).\\\\n          Hardware: 2× GPU on westd-19183.\\\\n\\\\n      - name: \\\\\\\"3.2 Vision Encoder Dominance (Claim 1)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        core_evidence:\\\\n          - d027_siglip_only_rsa          # SigLIP RSA=0.615 [CI: 0.526, 0.695]\\\\n          - lg_rsa_analysis               # VLA conditions 0.48-0.49\\\\n          - dinov2_baseline               # DINO RSA=0.388\\\\n          - p0_functional_probe_v2        # DINO R²=0.712 ≈ Proj 0.706 (p=0.22)\\\\n          - p0_functional_action_probe    # Proj R²=0.706\\\\n          - p0_functional_action_delta_probe  # Delta: Proj 0.118 ≈ LLM 0.122\\\\n        key_numbers:\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"SigLIP+Proj RSA=0.615 [CI: 0.526, 0.695]\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Full VLA RSA ≈ 0.50 (image_mean, all 4 conditions)\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"DINO RSA=0.388\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"LLM degradation: RSA gap = 0.615 − 0.501 = 0.114\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"DINO action R²=0.712 ≈ Projector R²=0.706 (Δ=0.005, p=0.22, n.s.)\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Geometric-functional dissociation: DINO=Proj in R², DINO<<Proj in RSA\\\\\\\"\\\\n        structure: >\\\\n          (a) RSA comparison: projector >> VLA >> DINO.\\\\n          (b) Functional probe: projector R²=0.706, confirming informational richness.\\\\n          (c) DINO control: R²≈Proj but RSA<<Proj → VL pretraining provides geometric alignment,\\\\n              not extra information content. Key dissociation finding.\\\\n          (d) Action-delta: all ~0.12, > DINO 0.06-0.10. SigLIP is source.\\\\n\\\\n      - name: \\\\\\\"3.3 LLM Redistributes Action Structure (Claim 2)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        core_evidence:\\\\n          - plan_003_perlayer_degradation  # Per-layer RSA curve\\\\n          - p0_functional_action_probe     # image_mean R²=0.683 < Proj 0.706; readout 0.731\\\\n          - p0_image_mean_rsa_verify       # image_mean RSA=0.501\\\\n          - untrained_last_preaction_probe  # untrained readout R²=0.686 < Proj 0.706\\\\n          - plan_009_cka_verification      # CKA: monotonic increase, ordering reversal\\\\n        key_numbers:\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"RSA degradation: 0.615 → 0.501 (image_mean)\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"R² degradation: 0.706 → 0.683 (CI=[0.011, 0.037], p=0.001)\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Readout concentration: R²=0.731 (CI vs proj: p=0.001)\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Untrained readout: R²=0.686 < projector 0.706\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Trained vs untrained readout: Δ=0.045, CI=[0.032, 0.058], p<0.0001\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Per-layer: Proj→L1 biggest drop (0.13-0.25), non-monotonic middle layers\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"CKA: monotonic increase L0→L32, last_preaction CKA=0.127 (highest)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        structure: >\\\\n          (a) Distributed degradation: image_mean RSA and R² both decrease through LLM.\\\\n          (b) Per-layer curve: non-monotonic, embedding interface is bottleneck.\\\\n          (c) Readout concentration: last_preaction R²=0.731 > projector.\\\\n          (d) Training-dependence: untrained readout 0.686 < projector 0.706.\\\\n              Trained readout 0.731 >> untrained 0.686 (Δ=0.045, p<0.0001).\\\\n          (e) CKA complement: global structure preserved (monotonic CKA),\\\\n              RSA-CKA dissociation as finding (Sec 3.5 or Discussion).\\\\n\\\\n      - name: \\\\\\\"3.4 Training = Routing, Not Creating (Claim 3)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        core_evidence:\\\\n          - p0_bootstrap_ci_training_effect  # image_mean CI includes 0\\\\n          - d021_instruction_ablation        # inst effect Δ=0.004\\\\n          - d027_trained_no_inst_rsa         # full 2×2 matrix\\\\n          - untrained_last_preaction_probe   # readout Δ=0.045 significant\\\\n          - d021_output_head_analysis        # vision backbone identical\\\\n          - plan_008_random_inst_control     # RUNNING — random instruction control\\\\n        key_numbers:\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Training effect on image_mean RSA: Δ=0.021, CI=[−0.031, +0.064], p=0.20 (n.s.)\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Training effect on last_preaction RSA: Δ=0.008, CI=[−0.040, +0.056], p=0.37 (n.s.)\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Instruction effect: Δ=0.004, far below threshold\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Training effect on readout R²: Δ=0.045, CI=[0.032, 0.058], p<0.0001 (significant)\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"2×2 matrix: all 4 conditions within 0.48-0.50 band\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Vision backbone + projector: identical weights (cos_sim=1.0) between trained/untrained\\\\\\\"\\\\n        structure: >\\\\n          (a) 2×2 RSA matrix: trained ≈ untrained, with-inst ≈ no-inst (all n.s.).\\\\n          (b) Bootstrap CI: distributed structure is training- and instruction-invariant.\\\\n          (c) Readout concentration IS training-dependent (Δ=0.045, p<0.0001).\\\\n          (d) Interpretation: training teaches LLM to route existing information to readout,\\\\n              does not create new action-relevant structure.\\\\n          (e) Random instruction control (plan_008, if available): further confirms instruction invariance.\\\\n\\\\n      - name: \\\\\\\"3.5 Metric Sensitivity: RSA vs CKA (Supplementary or Main)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        core_evidence:\\\\n          - plan_009_cka_verification\\\\n        key_numbers:\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"Linear CKA: untrained(0.089) > trained(0.083) > proj(0.081) > DINO(0.066)\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"RDM CKA: last_preaction(0.290) > proj(0.257) ≈ trained(0.260) > DINO(0.241)\\\\\\\"\\\\n          - \\\\\\\"CKA per-layer: monotonic increase (vs RSA non-monotonic)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        structure: >\\\\n          RSA vs CKA ordering reversal for projector vs trained.\\\\n          RSA = local geometric alignment; CKA = global structure preservation.\\\\n          Claim 2 explicitly scoped to \\\\\\\"geometric (RSA) degradation.\\\\\\\"\\\\n          CKA last_preaction highest — consistent with redistribution.\\\\n          Could be main text subsection or supplementary depending on space.\\\\n\\\\n  - name: Discussion\\\\n    structure:\\\\n      - para_1_synthesis: >\\\\n          Three findings form coherent picture: vision encoder is the source,\\\\n          LLM is the router, training teaches the routing policy.\\\\n          Projector provides 96.6% of LLM's functional capacity (0.706/0.731).\\\\n      - para_2_geometric_functional_dissociation: >\\\\n          DINO ≈ Projector in R² but << in RSA. Action information (decodability) is\\\\n          separate from geometric alignment (structural match to action space).\\\\n          VL pretraining (SigLIP) provides alignment, not extra information.\\\\n          Implications for encoder selection in VLA design.\\\\n      - para_3_rsa_cka_dissociation: >\\\\n          RSA and CKA capture different aspects — degradation in local geometry\\\\n          coexists with preservation/improvement in global structure.\\\\n          LLM transforms, not destroys. Metric choice determines narrative.\\\\n      - para_4_implications: >\\\\n          (1) Vision encoder pretraining quality is primary lever for action representations.\\\\n          (2) For tasks depending on distributed representations, use projector output directly.\\\\n          (3) LLM's value is readout concentration — relevant for action head design.\\\\n          (4) Language instructions do not modulate representation structure — rethink instruction role.\\\\n      - para_5_connection_to_behavioral: >\\\\n          Our representational findings explain Grant et al. 2026's behavioral results:\\\\n          vision dominates because the frozen encoder provides the action-relevant structure.\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Encoded but not used\\\\\\\" (their instruction finding) aligns with our instruction invariance.\\\\n      - para_6_limitations: >\\\\n          Single architecture (OpenVLA). Single benchmark suite (LIBERO).\\\\n          RSA captures structural alignment, not causal contribution.\\\\n          plan_012 CLIP-ViT control would strengthen encoder comparison.\\\\n    core_evidence:\\\\n      - p0_functional_probe_v2   # DINO ≈ Proj dissociation\\\\n      - plan_009_cka_verification  # RSA-CKA dissociation\\\\n      - d021_instruction_ablation  # instruction invariance\\\\n\\\\n  - name: Conclusion\\\\n    structure:\\\\n      - para_1_summary: >\\\\n          First representational decomposition of a VLA. Three findings:\\\\n          (1) frozen vision encoder dominates action-relevant structure,\\\\n          (2) LLM redistributes via training-dependent readout concentration,\\\\n          (3) training teaches routing, not creating.\\\\n      - para_2_takeaway: >\\\\n          Action representations in VLAs are inherited, not learned.\\\\n          Design implications: invest in vision encoder pretraining,\\\\n          not in larger LLMs or more fine-tuning data for action knowledge.\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# FIGURES\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\nfigures:\\\\n  - id: fig1\\\\n    type: \\\\\\\"概念图/架构图\\\\\\\"\\\\n    description: >\\\\n      VLA pipeline decomposition diagram. Shows SigLIP → Projector → LLM (32 layers) → Action Head.\\\\n      Annotate extraction points: (a) projector output, (b) per-layer image_mean, (c) per-layer\\\\n      last_preaction (readout token). Show 2×2 conditions (trained/untrained × with/no instruction).\\\\n      Inset: RSA and functional probe methodology schematic.\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"architectural (no experiment data)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: \\\\\\\"Section 2 or Section 1\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: fig2\\\\n    type: \\\\\\\"实验结果图 — Bar chart\\\\\\\"\\\\n    description: >\\\\n      Main RSA comparison. Bars: SigLIP+Projector (0.615), VLA trained w-inst (0.501),\\\\n      VLA untrained w-inst (0.485), VLA trained no-inst (0.501), VLA untrained no-inst (0.482),\\\\n      DINO (0.388). Error bars from bootstrap CI. Clear visual gap between projector and all VLA conditions.\\\\n    data_source:\\\\n      - d027_siglip_only_rsa\\\\n      - p0_image_mean_rsa_verify\\\\n      - d027_trained_no_inst_rsa\\\\n      - d021_instruction_ablation\\\\n    placement: \\\\\\\"Section 3.2\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: fig3\\\\n    type: \\\\\\\"实验结果图 — Line plot\\\\\\\"\\\\n    description: >\\\\n      Per-layer RSA degradation curves. X-axis: Projector → L0 → L1 → ... → L32.\\\\n      Y-axis: RSA with action space. Four lines: trained w-inst, trained no-inst,\\\\n      untrained w-inst, untrained no-inst. Horizontal reference line at projector (0.615)\\\\n      and DINO (0.388). Shows non-monotonic pattern, Proj→L1 major drop,\\\\n      partial mid-layer recovery.\\\\n    data_source:\\\\n      - plan_003_perlayer_degradation\\\\n    placement: \\\\\\\"Section 3.3\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: fig4\\\\n    type: \\\\\\\"实验结果图 — Grouped bar chart\\\\\\\"\\\\n    description: >\\\\n      Functional probe R² comparison. Groups: Projector, LLM image_mean (trained),\\\\n      LLM readout (trained), LLM readout (untrained), DINO.\\\\n      Shows: (a) image_mean < projector (degradation), (b) trained readout > projector (concentration),\\\\n      (c) untrained readout < projector (redistribution requires training),\\\\n      (d) DINO ≈ projector (geometric-functional dissociation).\\\\n      Error bars from bootstrap. Significance brackets for key comparisons.\\\\n    data_source:\\\\n      - p0_functional_action_probe\\\\n      - p0_functional_probe_v2\\\\n      - untrained_last_preaction_probe\\\\n    placement: \\\\\\\"Section 3.3\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: fig5\\\\n    type: \\\\\\\"实验结果图 — 2×2 heatmap or matrix plot\\\\\\\"\\\\n    description: >\\\\n      Training × Instruction effect matrix. 2×2 grid showing RSA values for\\\\n      trained/untrained × with/no-inst. All values in narrow 0.48-0.50 band.\\\\n      Bootstrap CI annotations. Visual message: training and instruction have negligible effect\\\\n      on distributed structure.\\\\n    data_source:\\\\n      - d027_trained_no_inst_rsa\\\\n      - p0_bootstrap_ci_training_effect\\\\n    placement: \\\\\\\"Section 3.4\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: fig6\\\\n    type: \\\\\\\"实验结果图 — Dual-axis or panel plot (supplementary candidate)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    description: >\\\\n      RSA vs CKA per-layer comparison. Two panels or dual Y-axis.\\\\n      RSA: non-monotonic, degradation from projector. CKA: monotonic increase.\\\\n      Shows metric-dependent interpretation. CKA last_preaction as outlier (highest).\\\\n    data_source:\\\\n      - plan_009_cka_verification\\\\n      - plan_003_perlayer_degradation\\\\n      - untrained_last_preaction_probe\\\\n    placement: \\\\\\\"Section 3.5 or Supplementary\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# TABLES\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\ntables:\\\\n  - id: tab1\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"Main RSA results — component comparison with bootstrap CI\\\\\\\"\\\\n    columns: [\\\\\\\"Model/Condition\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Aggregation\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Peak Layer\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"RSA ρ\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"95% CI\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    rows_outline:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"SigLIP+Projector | — | — | 0.615 | [0.526, 0.695]\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"VLA Trained w-inst | image_mean | L23 | 0.501 | TBD\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"VLA Trained no-inst | image_mean | L23 | 0.501 | TBD\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"VLA Untrained w-inst | image_mean | L23 | 0.485 | TBD\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"VLA Untrained no-inst | image_mean | L23 | 0.482 | TBD\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"DINOv2 | cls_token | — | 0.388 | TBD\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source:\\\\n      - d027_siglip_only_rsa\\\\n      - p0_image_mean_rsa_verify\\\\n      - d027_trained_no_inst_rsa\\\\n      - d021_instruction_ablation\\\\n    placement: \\\\\\\"Section 3.2\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: tab2\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"Functional action probe R² — component decomposition\\\\\\\"\\\\n    columns: [\\\\\\\"Representation\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Raw Action R²\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Action-Delta R²\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Notes\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    rows_outline:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"SigLIP+Projector | 0.706 | 0.118 | Vision encoder output\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"VLA Trained image_mean L19 | 0.683 | 0.122 | Degraded vs Proj (p=0.001)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"VLA Trained readout L19 | 0.731 | TBD | Concentrated (p=0.001 vs Proj)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"VLA Untrained image_mean L0 | 0.714 | 0.117 | Architecture-intrinsic\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"VLA Untrained readout L32 | 0.686 | TBD | < Proj → redistribution requires training\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"DINOv2 cls_token | 0.712 | 0.100 | ≈ Proj in R² (p=0.22), << in RSA\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source:\\\\n      - p0_functional_action_probe\\\\n      - p0_functional_action_delta_probe\\\\n      - p0_functional_probe_v2\\\\n      - untrained_last_preaction_probe\\\\n    placement: \\\\\\\"Section 3.3\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: tab3\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"Bootstrap CI for training and instruction effects\\\\\\\"\\\\n    columns: [\\\\\\\"Comparison\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Aggregation\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Δ\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"95% CI\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"p-value\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Significant?\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    rows_outline:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Training (image_mean RSA) | image_mean | +0.021 | [−0.031, +0.064] | 0.20 | No\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Training (last_preaction RSA) | last_preaction | +0.008 | [−0.040, +0.056] | 0.37 | No\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Training (readout R²) | last_preaction | +0.045 | [+0.032, +0.058] | <0.0001 | Yes\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Instruction (RSA) | — | +0.004 | — | — | No\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Proj vs LLM image_mean (R²) | image_mean | +0.023 | [+0.011, +0.037] | 0.001 | Yes\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Trained readout vs Proj (R²) | last_preaction | +0.025 | — | 0.001 | Yes\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source:\\\\n      - p0_bootstrap_ci_training_effect\\\\n      - untrained_last_preaction_probe\\\\n      - p0_functional_probe_v2\\\\n    placement: \\\\\\\"Section 3.4\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# WRITING ORDER\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\nwriting_order:\\\\n  - section: \\\\\\\"Experiments (Sec 3)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    reason: \\\\\\\"Evidence-first: establish all claims with data before writing narrative\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - section: \\\\\\\"Method (Sec 2)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    reason: \\\\\\\"Formalize methodology after experiments are written — ensures method matches what was actually done\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - section: \\\\\\\"Discussion\\\\\\\"\\\\n    reason: \\\\\\\"Synthesize implications after results are on paper\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - section: \\\\\\\"Introduction\\\\\\\"\\\\n    reason: \\\\\\\"Now the story arc is clear — write intro that sets up the exact findings\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - section: \\\\\\\"Related Work\\\\\\\"\\\\n    reason: \\\\\\\"Position against literature with full knowledge of our contributions\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - section: \\\\\\\"Conclusion\\\\\\\"\\\\n    reason: \\\\\\\"Compress the story\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - section: \\\\\\\"Abstract\\\\\\\"\\\\n    reason: \\\\\\\"Last — summarizes the complete paper\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# LITERATURE STRATEGY\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\nliterature_strategy:\\\\n  must_cite:\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Kim et al. 2024 — OpenVLA (model we analyze)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Karamcheti et al. 2024 — Prismatic (OpenVLA's architectural foundation)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Grant et al. 2026 — behavioral visual dominance in VLAs\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Brohan et al. 2023 — RT-2 (VLA paradigm)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Hou 2026 (LangGap) — VLAs ignore language\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Fei 2025 (LIBERO-Plus) — language-conditioned evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Haon et al. CoRL 2025 — activation steering OpenVLA\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Liu et al. 2024 (LIBERO) — benchmark\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Kriegeskorte et al. 2008 — RSA methodology\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Zhai et al. 2023 — SigLIP\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Oquab et al. 2024 — DINOv2\\\\\\\"\\\\n  compare_with:\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Grant et al. 2026 — behavioral vs representational evidence (complementary)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Lu et al. 2025 — linear probing OpenVLA (differentiate: component decomposition vs layer probing)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Haon et al. CoRL 2025 — activation steering (complementary: sparse directions vs geometric analysis)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Swann et al. 2026 — SAE interpretability (complementary: feature-level vs geometric-level)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Fang 2025 — fine-tuning erodes reasoning (related but different scope: VLM vs VLA)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Molinari et al. 2025 — emergent world representations (complementary: world models vs action structure)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Rizzardo et al. 2026 — feature observability (complementary: controllability vs decomposition)\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# PENDING / CONTINGENT\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\npending:\\\\n  - exp_id: plan_008_random_inst_control\\\\n    status: RUNNING\\\\n    impact: \\\\\\\"Strengthens Claim 3 instruction invariance. If significant → revise Claim 3 language.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    fallback: \\\\\\\"Claim 3 already supported by 2×2 matrix; random-inst is supplementary confirmation.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - exp_id: plan_012_clip_vit_control\\\\n    status: PLANNED\\\\n    impact: \\\\\\\"Distinguishes 'VL pretraining' from 'SigLIP specifically' for Claim 1.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    fallback: \\\\\\\"Discussion acknowledges confound; DINO ≈ Proj dissociation partially addresses.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 24330, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01G9mp9xmjhnKJ393kxGNeHB","session_id":"eb31c56b-0827-4300-bb16-02d899856f0f","uuid":"c7159100-3b9a-4a45-9ed9-e22f87c8a5b6","timestamp":"2026-05-16T01:55:22.154Z"}
